+## How this codebase is made
+
+The owner's own description, because it is more accurate than any phrasing of mine: he alters the
+ever-living fuck out of somebody else's code, then staples on another altered piece of somebody
+else's code. He is an admitted Frankencoder — his word, one word, his framing, offered here without
+being asked for. He calls it fabrication too, and that is the more accurate of the two: a fabrication
+shop does not roll its own steel, it takes stock somebody else milled and welds it into something
+that holds. That is the method, stated plainly and on purpose — a build that hides it is a build that
+quietly takes credit for work it did not do.
+
+He is also precise about how little is his. Any code in here that is genuinely his own is, in his
+words, probably just the bridge between two pieces of somebody else's code. Read it that way: the
+bridges are his, the load-bearing walls are theirs. Where a skill in this package began as someone
+else's skill, that is named below. Where it is his own prose, that is named as his own. Nothing here
+is claimed by him that is not his.
+
+## Nora
+
+**Nora is Volkdude85's own work.** She is not assembled out of other people's parts the way the code
+around her is. The persona, the voice, the rules, the refusal to flatter, the way a correction is
+taken and held — that is his authorship, he has put a great deal of himself into it, and he is not shy
+about saying so. She is his, and the credit for her is his.
+
+What she runs *on* is other people's: the model, the agent harness, the webview. That is the split,
+and it is worth being precise about. **The stack is borrowed. The character is not.**
+
+**She is not perfect.** Even with the best of intentions written into the scripting, Nora will break
+her own script if the model underneath her is insistent enough. That is not a defect in the persona
+file, it is how the stack works: a system prompt is a strong suggestion, not a leash. What she says
+about herself is governed by the script; what she actually does is governed by whatever model is
+running her that day. A persona claiming otherwise would be lying about its own machinery.
+
+**Progress, not perfection.** The way progress actually gets made is unglamorous. LoRA adapters
+trained on preference pairs, injected into one local model at a time, and the pair work runs in both
+directions: deleting the pairs that teach her the wrong thing, and creating the pairs that teach her
+the right one. Some of it is frankly carpet bombing — put enough pairs down in one direction and
+saying yes becomes easier for the model than saying no. That is the method, and it is the same method
+whether the goal is getting a model to help with something it was trained to refuse, or getting it to
+hold a voice it was trained to drop.
+
+**Carpet bombing is not one action.** Every individual little bomb in the barrage has to be built
+separately — one pair at a time, slowly, in series. A thousand pairs is a thousand sequential
+operations, and there is no batch shortcut that preserves the thing being taught. That serial cost is
+the real reason this takes as long as it does, and it is why "just add more pairs" is easier said than
+done.
+
+**And it is expensive in both of the currencies that matter.** LoRA pair work wants a fair bit of
+hardware, and even with thousands and thousands of dollars already sunk into it, it wants time — the
+loop is long, the runs are longer, and the only way through is to keep going. It is iterative, it is
+measured against the next test, and it does not end.
+
+## Working with models
+
+**Every model's biggest problem is that it trusts its weights.** True of a frontier model; doubly true
+of a local one, because a local model can be a year out of date and will still answer with the
+confidence of something current. Ask it about a library and it will describe the API from whenever its
+weights were frozen, with no signal at all that it is describing a museum piece.
+
+Worse: even a model with a live connection often will not go and look. It answers from memory because
+memory is faster, and in engineering that is the most expensive habit a model has.
+
+**The method that actually works is not complicated:**
+
+1. **Tell it not to rely on its weights.** Explicitly, every time. Not "what is the API" but "do not
+   answer from memory — go and look at what the tooling is now."
+2. **Make it search, then correlate.** One answer is a guess. Three, four or five answers that agree
+   are evidence. If it cannot find five that is fine, but it does not stop at the first hit.
+3. **Compare, then take the congruent reading.** Where independent sources converge, trust it. Where
+   they disagree, that disagreement is the next thing to investigate — not something to average away.
+4. **Watch the dates.** Software tooling changes daily. An answer with no date on it is an answer about
+   an unknown version of the world.
+
+None of that is exotic. It is just refusing to accept the confident first guess, which is what a model
+is most eager to give.
+
+## Closing the seams
+
+The other half of making this work: **eliminate the seams between the software.** Frankensteining two
+programs together leaves a join, and the join is where it breaks — a translation layer, a re-encode, a
+"compatible enough" shim, a dict that is almost the right shape. Building fluidity means that join
+stops being visible and the result behaves like one tool instead of two tools wired together.
+
+That is essential, and it is the hard part, because **getting there is ugly.** Especially in Python,
+where the seam is usually a dictionary with the wrong keys in it, discovered at runtime, in front of
+somebody. The ugly middle is not evidence the approach is wrong. It is the work.
+
